 | close | `./zn close <issue> --reason "<why>" [--carry]` | in a worktree (refused in the main checkout). Closes an issue as won't do: publishes one tracker-only commit (`tracker: close <id> won't do`, with a `Reason:` line) that stamps its `status:` `closed-…` and adds `wont_do: <why>` as the frontmatter's last line, judged on `origin/main`; the working tree sees it on its next `./zn sync`. `--carry` writes the same edit in this tree, judged on this tree, and publishes nothing (needs no origin): the branch's pull request lands it. It refuses a raft id (`./zn unfold` and `./zn recombine` retire a raft), an unknown id, a done or readied issue (`./zn stop <raft>` first), one already closed, one being prepared (preparing: its holder runs `./zn status <id> new`), one in a raft (`./zn unfold <raft> <id>`), and one named in the `depends_on` of a live (backlog or active, not closed) item; each refusal names its way out, and a missing, blank or multi-line reason is refused first. A closed issue keeps its folder: `./zn find` and `./zn status <id>` (`closed`) still answer, `./zn list closed` shows it with its reason, and `list`, the bare `status` listing, the board, `raft-plan` and the dispatcher leave it out. Nothing moves it again (c0278). |
-| approve | `./zn approve <issue-id> [--check]` | for a prepared issue, in the worktree where the design is; for an active one, in the raft's worktree on its top layer. It is where approval is given; `done` and `finish` check it, with a terminal on stdin, and not while `ZN_DISPATCH` is set. Records a hash of the design sections (Problem, Solution, Design, Acceptance criteria, Test plan), the acceptance specs and `check.sh` in the issue's `approval.md`, and keeps earlier rows. `--check` says whether that hash still holds and which part changed. |
+| approve | `./zn approve <issue-id> [--check\|--publish]` | one command, with a terminal on stdin, and not while `ZN_DISPATCH` is set. It is where approval is given; `done` and `finish` check it. **It finds the design itself**, from the main checkout or any worktree: the worktree of the issue's prep branch (`prep-<slug>`), or the worktree the command is run in, when it holds the issue `design: ready` with a contract of its own (not `origin/main`'s, not the one it branched from, not one `origin/main` held since) and no matching approval; else `origin/main`, when the design is merged there with no matching approval. For an issue in `active/` on `origin/main` its one place is its raft's worktree (`.worktree/<R-id>`), found from anywhere: it records only when that worktree is on the raft's top layer with a clean tree, and pushes that layer, never forced (the approval lands with the raft; `approval.md` counts as written after the layer's finish, so the layer stays finished for `./zn done`); otherwise it refuses, naming the top layer's branch, never a directory. No other worktree is read, so a prototype or a copy is approved only by running the command inside it. It says where it acts, records there and makes the approval count in the same command. It records a hash of the design sections (Problem, Solution, Design, Acceptance criteria, Test plan), the acceptance specs, `check.sh` and `guesses.md` in the issue's `approval.md`, and keeps earlier rows. A design that is `design: ready` on `origin/main` with the same contract is published (this row appended to `origin/main`'s `approval.md`, one commit), any other is pushed on its branch and counts when its pull request merges. Only the prep branch, or a branch already on origin, is pushed, and never forced; a branch that is not on origin is left as it is (the record counts when its design reaches `origin/main`), and a raft's layer branch is pushed, never forced. An `approval.md` on `origin/main` that does not parse is replaced by the approval (its rows cannot be read); a worktree on no branch is refused before anything is recorded. While a worktree it reads holds a design of its own, `origin/main`'s older design is not approved behind it. A merged design is recorded in a detached worktree the command makes at `.worktree/.approve-<id>` and removes once published; the main checkout is never written. An approval a worktree already holds for the design `origin/main` holds is published as it is, never recorded twice. When the publish or push fails, or publishing is off, the record stays, the command fails saying it does not count yet, and `./zn approve <id> --publish` retries it from anywhere. `--check` reads the checkout it runs in: it says whether that hash still holds and which part changed. |
 | ready | `./zn ready <R-id\|issue-id>` | main checkout only, on a clean `main`, with an origin (z-ready skill). Checks the raft and every member are `design: ready` with a `check.sh` that has a check, and that every `depends_on` outside the raft is `done`. Does not ask for approval (`./zn done` does), except under `./zn dispatch`. `--on <R-id>` readies a raft built on a raft that has not landed: its first layer is cut from `<R-id>/base`, and the ready commit carries the trailers `Lands: approved` and `On: <R-id>`. Claims the raft, publishes one tracker-only commit that moves it and its members to `active/` on `origin/main` (every `check.sh` item path rewritten), pulls the main checkout, and writes the ignored `<root>/rafts/active/<R-id>/prompt.md` (it inlines the project's `<root>/architecture.md`, pickman's map; a project without one gets a line saying so). On an active raft it regenerates the prompt and makes no commit. |
 | stop | `./zn stop <R-id>` | main checkout only, on a clean `main`, with an origin (z-stop skill). It gives back exactly the issues that carry the raft's own claim stamp (`ready` and `start` stamp the raft and every issue they claim with one `active-…` value); an issue only listed in `issues:`, or one filed naming the raft after the claim, is never touched (the latter is named in a warning). Every claim stamp is new on `origin/main` (redrawn until no item carries it), and a raft with no `active-…` stamp is refused, to be given back by hand. Gives back a readied raft: publishes one tracker-only commit that moves it and its members from `active/` back to the backlog, stamped `prepared-…` (every `check.sh` item path rewritten back), removes `prompt.md`, pulls the main checkout, and names the raft's branch and PR, which it leaves as they are. A raft claimed by the legacy `start` (backlog, status `active`) gets a status-only publish and no move. A member in `done/` refuses. |
 | start | `./zn start R0010` | clean tree required. Opens the next layer (`R0010/1/c0010`: an empty `tracker: start` commit, a push, and a **draft** PR based on the layer below, or on main). The next layer waits until `./zn finish` has made the one below a single commit. A readied raft claims and moves nothing. A backlog raft (legacy) still claims and moves to `active/` on that layer branch: members must be `design: ready`, and every `depends_on` outside the raft must be `done` on `origin/main`. If it refuses, merge `origin/main` and retry, never edit `depends_on` to get past it: stop and report. Do this first, so the claim is visible to another machine. |
